WEBVTT
TITLE Memory Interfacing and Interleaving — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A memory chip is a rectangle: a number of words, each a fixed number of bits wide. It is written words times bits.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The memory the design needs is a bigger rectangle: N words of B bits. Covering it takes two separate divisions.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Width first: chips across equals capital B over small b. Depth second: rows equals capital N over small W. Total is the product.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Sixteen K words of sixteen bits from one K words of eight bits: two chips across, sixteen rows, thirty-two chips.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The total number of address bits is the base-two logarithm of the total number of words in the finished memory.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Each chip consumes the logarithm of its own word count. Whatever is left over has to choose a row.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Four leftover bits drive a four-to-sixteen decoder: fourteen minus ten is four, and two to the four is sixteen rows.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Check the split against itself: two to the leftover bits must equal the number of rows, and the two bit counts must add to the total.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Inside one row every chip receives the same low-order address bits, because they all serve the same word at once.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The leftover bits go to the decoder, whose outputs are the chip-select pins: exactly one row is enabled per address.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The decoded bits need not be the top ones. GATE 2023 sent IA4 and IA3 to the decoder and kept the rest for the chips.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So the four blocks start at zero, eight, sixteen and twenty-four, and each owns eight bytes out of every thirty-two.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A chip-select pin is a logic condition on address bits: an AND gate whose inputs are bits or their complements.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Fix k bits out of n and two to the n minus k addresses remain. That count is the size of the range.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
GATE 2019: A15, A14 and A11 must be one; A13 and A12 must be zero. That is C800 up to CFFF.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The size of a chip-select region is set by the bits that were not decoded, not by the size of the chip behind it.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The decoder a design needs is usually not the decoder in the parts bin, so it is built from smaller ones.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
One two-to-four decoder takes the top two bits and enables four more, each of which takes the bottom two bits.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The count is the sum of the tree: one plus four is five two-to-four decoders.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Two traps live here: counting chips where the question counts rows, and forgetting that only the row count sets the decoder.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Interleaving splits memory into m banks. Each bank is busy for a whole cycle after it accepts a request.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Low-order interleaving takes the bank from the low bits: bank equals address mod m, so consecutive words go to different banks.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
High-order interleaving takes the bank from the high bits, so consecutive words stay inside one bank until it runs out.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Only low-order interleaving turns a sequential stream into parallel work, and that is the entire point of the design.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
With m banks the bus can start a new word every bus cycle, provided a bank is free when its turn comes.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
A bank is free again after its cycle time, and the bus comes back to it after m bus cycles. Compare the two.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
So the clean formula holds exactly when m times the bus cycle is at least the bank cycle. Otherwise the pipeline stalls.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Two banks, forty nanoseconds each, ten nanosecond bus: eight words take a hundred and seventy, not a hundred and ten.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Bandwidth is the word width divided by the steady interval between words, and the interval is the larger of the bus cycle and the cycle time over m.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Interleaving multiplies bandwidth by m only until the bus saturates, and after that more banks buy nothing.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
GATE 2014: eight modules busy for six hundred nanoseconds behind a hundred nanosecond bus. The bus is the limit, so ten thousand stores in a millisecond.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
With five modules instead of eight the answer would be eight thousand three hundred and thirty, because the bus would stall.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
GATE 2006: twenty-four banks, each two bytes wide, so one parallel access delivers forty-eight bytes.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
A sixty-four byte cache block needs two rounds, and ninety-six bytes travel to deliver sixty-four.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Each round costs the bank latency of eighty nanoseconds plus twelve for decoding the bank numbers, so ninety-two.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The answer most keys give is a hundred and eighty-four nanoseconds. Some published solutions say a hundred and seventy-two, and the lesson says so.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: divide twice. Bits for the width, words for the depth. Never one division for both.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: address bits are log of the words, the chip takes log of its words, and the rest go to the decoder.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: for interleaving, write the bank schedule, then check m times the bus cycle against the bank cycle.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: convert units once, at the end, and keep the five traps in mind.

